The '''''Tsuwasha''''' is a river of northern [[Louyang]], running west to east across the region south of [[Sinchew]]. It is approximately 1,244 km long.

The river rises in the [[Great Lurandian Mountain Range]] in the east and flows westward, discharging into the [[Gulf of Louyang]].

== Etymology ==
Tsuwasha comes from the [[Hachinawa|Hahkina]], from ''tsu'', "south", and ''washa'', "water" as it signified their southern boundary.

== Place in the colonial period ==

The Tsuwasha is significant as the southern boundary of the territory recognised as [[Hachinawa|Hahkina]] by the [[Treaty of Anping]] of 1538.

South of the river lay further [[Mahik]] peoples, known to the Hahkina as the southern mahik — distinct from the Kawesa chiefdoms on the Hahkina's northern frontier. The Hahkina therefore bordered mahik peoples on two sides, separated from the southern group by the Tsuwasha.

The fixing of the Tsuwasha as a boundary was the origin of the Hahkina state's defined frontier. Before the treaty the Hahkina asserted authority over the lands of their tributaries and neighbours without delimitation; the agreement obliged them to accept a bounded territory with a named limit, in the manner of a state rather than a sphere of influence. It is among the earliest recorded instances in the region of an indigenous polity acquiring a delimited frontier.

The river lies entirely within modern [[Louyang]].

